## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Practices and concept areas | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Using our tools well. *What are the parts of our devices for, and what is my job in keeping them, and us, safe?* Anchor: a class device that "won't work" because a classmate left it signed in, with a juice box beside it and its headphones unplugged | 6 | g1-responsible-use; g1-hardware-parts; g1-keep-safe | E1-SYS-IM-13; E1-SYS-HW-11; E1-SYS-SE-12 | Concept area: systems and security. Practices: ESR1 and IC3 (why a rule helps everyone, said to a partner); IC3 (each part named and its job explained); ESR2 and IC5 (keeping a password private, signing out for the next pair); HCD10 (parts that help different users) | Session 1: the kindergarten device rules retrieved after the hook; why each rule exists, the child's own part in keeping it, and what happens to the child and to the class when it is broken (a dropped device no one can use, a drink near a keyboard, using someone else's sign-in, sound played aloud instead of in headphones), acted out with story cards. Session 2: the parts of a device named with their right words and their purposes on picture cards (screen, keyboard, mouse or touchpad, speaker, headphones, camera, microphone), and that a laptop or tablet runs the apps we use for school work; adapted parts that do the same jobs for different people (a large-button mouse, a switch, a magnifier). Session 3 (device): each pair matches part cards to the parts of a real device and says each one's job, then watches the teacher sign out of the class device on one screen. Session 4: a password is a private key to an account, a lock is its picture; children sign in and out of a paper pretend login with a given pretend password, sort what is fine to share (a drawing) and what stays private (a password, a sign-in picture code), and say what to do if someone asks for it (say no, tell a trusted adult). Out: no internal parts (grade 2 adds input and output, and inside parts come later), no input or output sorting, no child makes up a password and no real account (the boundary asks neither), no copyright, misinformation or digital footprint. End-of-unit (day 5): a new part-and-job card match with one part named and its purpose said aloud (E1-SYS-HW-11); a new rule scene, saying why the rule matters and what happens to self and class if it is broken (E1-SYS-IM-13); a new pretend login card, circling what stays secret and the sign-out button, and saying what to do if asked for it (E1-SYS-SE-12); respond day 6. Tools: device stations (any school device, its home screen only, job: matching parts to jobs; the teacher's sign-out shown on one screen). Curio Class writes: the part-and-job picture cards with adapted parts, the rule story cards, the paper pretend login and the share-or-private sort set | First because the rules and the parts' names must exist before the year's first device turn (session 3) and every device day of unit 3; responsible use opens the unit so the device turn happens under rules the class has just explained. Keep-safe sits with the rules as a rule for keeping our information safe, taught on paper. Base: archived Year 2 "IT around us" lessons 1 and 5 | Candidate: kept, a station rotation plan for a room with three devices on session 3, zero added days | Some children use adapted devices or tools: talk is about what a part lets a person do, never about a classmate; use the wording the sensitivity read settles from its pack. Some homes have no device and some have many: no child is asked what is at home. Passwords: no real password, name or address is said or written; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. Devices carried with two hands, cords behind the table, no food or drink near them, headphone volume set by the teacher. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 2 | Plans in parts. *How do we turn a big job into steps someone else can follow, and what can people and computers each do best?* Anchor: the class tries to build the block tower in the picture from one long spoken instruction, and it falls | 6 | g1-split-task; g1-change-steps; g1-patterns-ai; g1-people-or-machine | E1-ALG-PS-01; E1-ALG-IM-03; E1-ALG-ML-02; E1-SOC-HU-15 | Concept area: algorithms and design (with computing and society: people and computers compared). Practices: CT6 (cut the job into smaller parts); CT7 (notice the pattern in the examples); CT8 (plan the steps before carrying them out); ESR1 and IC3 (a changed order gives a different result, said to a partner); HCD10 (which jobs need a person) | Session 1: the step-card retrieved; a familiar multistep job (building the tower, tidying the art table, getting ready for recess) cut into its parts with the teacher, then each part into ordered step-cards, a worked example shown and traced first. Session 2: two step-card sets for the same tower, the steps in different orders; children predict and then build both, and say how the change of order changed the result (a tall steady tower, a short wobbly one). Session 3 (modify): a working plan from session 1 has one step swapped, dropped or added; children predict what will come out differently and check by carrying it out. Session 4 (make): each pair splits a new class job into parts and step-cards, and a partner pair carries the cards out and says whether they worked; the class keeps two plans (a path to the reading rug, a tower) for unit 3. Session 5: attribute cards sorted, then a guessing game: a partner who has seen a few sorted examples guesses a new card's group by the pattern; some computer systems, made by people, look for repeating patterns in many examples to make a choice or make something new (a library screen that suggests a next book from the books read before; a drawing tool that makes a new picture of a cat from the patterns in many cat pictures). Session 6: a sort of activity cards into what people do well (comfort a friend, invent a game, solve a new kind of problem), what a computer does well (add a long list fast, repeat a step without tiring or mistakes, keep a lot of information) and both, with one reason said for each. Out: no conditions (if) and no programming language in the algorithm work, no abstract problems, no kinds of machine learning, no claim that a computer thinks or feels, no machine at all on session 5, no repeat (grade 2), no ethics of who should do a job (later grades). End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 13) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the tower and class-job part and step-cards, the two-order tower sets, the attribute-card sets, the suggestion-screen picture, the people-or-computer activity cards | Second because every later algorithm and program row builds on the split task (g2-algorithm-loops), and unit 3's code is translated from the plans made here (g1-code-sequence's notes name g1-split-task). Change-steps follows the split so there is a plan to change, and the make session comes after the modify. Patterns and people-or-computer close the unit together: the guessing game shows what pattern-finding systems do, and the sort places that beside what people do well. After unit 1, so the year's device rules come first, though this unit needs no device. Base: archived Year 2 "Robot algorithms" lessons 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 for sessions 1 to 4 | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "same but different" pairing of two algorithms for one goal on session 2, zero added days | Feelings cards (comfort a friend, notice a mood) are about people in a story, never about a child in the room. Class jobs, not home jobs: home routines differ, and a home example is only a child's own offer. No food handled. The suggestion screen is a drawn, generic screen with no product name. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 3 | Code that waits for a tap. *How do we turn our plan into a program that starts when we tap it, and fix it when it goes wrong?* Anchor: the class's rug path from unit 2, built as picture blocks, does nothing until the teacher taps the start block, then the character walks one square too far | 8 | g1-changing-things; g1-code-sequence; g1-explain-events; g1-fix-sequence | E1-PRO-VD-05; E1-PRO-PD-04; E1-PRO-RD-06; E1-PRO-TR-07 | Concept area: programming. Practices: IC3 and CT7 (name what changes and what stays the same); CT7 and CT8 (turn a plan into blocks); IC3 and CT6 (say what each block does and what the tap starts); CT6 and CT9 (find the first wrong block, change one thing, test again); IC5 (driver and navigator roles that switch at the halfway signal) | Session 1: words for information that changes over time in everyday life (the weather, the temperature outside, the time, a plant's height, a game's score, our age each year) and information that stays the same (a name, a birthday), sorted on picture cards; the character's square changes as a program runs. The first idea of a changing value; the word variable is not taught. Session 2 (device): the worked example: the class's rug-path plan from unit 2 turned into picture blocks with one event block ("when tapped, start") on top, traced block by block and run on one screen, then each pair builds the same program from the plan (or the block-card paper path). Session 3 (device): a short given block program that starts on an event; children say what each block does and what the tap starts, before it runs, then run and compare. Session 4: a given program with one block wrong or out of order (the character jumps before it moves); trace it, find the first block that goes wrong, change one thing and test again: predict, run, find, fix; bug taught with both meanings; errors are normal and shared; on the floor grid with block cards, then checked on one screen. Session 5 (modify, device): a working program; the goal square moves; change one block and test again. Session 6 (make, device): each pair turns a given step-card plan (unit 2's tower or a new picture plan) into a block program with a start block, runs it, fixes what goes wrong and says what they tested. Out: no repeat or loop (grade 2), no conditions, no variables in code and no word variable, no designing the algorithm during the code sessions (the plan is given or made in unit 2), no reading beyond icons and short block labels, no text view for children (shown to the teacher only). End-of-unit (day 21): lay out block cards with a start card for a new given plan (E1-PRO-PD-04); say what a new event program does when tapped, block by block (E1-PRO-RD-06); circle the first wrong block in a new program and move it to fix it (E1-PRO-TR-07); sort new picture cards into changes and stays the same and name one changing thing (E1-PRO-VD-05); respond day 22. Tools: the picture-block tool (large picture blocks on a grid, a "when tapped" event block, a step-through run, job: build, run and fix the program the plan describes; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the block cards and start cards for the paper path, the changes-or-stays-the-same picture cards, the worked, buggy and moved-goal programs with their keys | After unit 2 because the code is translated from unit 2's plans (model_in step-card) and the block program needs the step-card first. The changing-value sort opens the unit so the character's moving square is seen as a value that changes before the code runs. Trace and predict come before fix, fix before modify, and modify before make (APPROACH.md order). Placed across weeks 15 to 22, which include the core check weeks 15, 16 and 19, because it holds no course-level check. Base: archived Year 2 "Programming quizzes" lessons 1 to 4 and 6, and "Robot algorithms" lesson 4 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "changing a design" step as session 5's modify task, zero added days | Floor grid in a cleared space, tape flat, children walk (no running, no blindfolds); a child who uses a wheelchair or cannot walk the grid moves the toy or directs a partner, never sits out. Age is a changing value: say "everyone's age changes each year", and no child's age or birthday is asked or shown. Left and right: hand signs and a shape on each block, never color alone. Devices: two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 4 | Counting, looking and asking. *How can we find out about our classroom, and which questions can finding out answer?* Anchor: two classmates argue about whether the class plant has grown, and nobody can say for sure | 4 | g1-two-kinds-data; g1-data-questions | E1-DAT-DC-08; E1-DAT-DI-09; E1-DAT-IM-10 | Concept area: data and analysis. Practices: IC3 and CT7 (say what the count and the description show); CT6 and IC3 (sort the questions and say why); ESR1 and HCD10 (who a question helps and who it leaves out) | Session 1: data collected in more than one way: counts by counting and measuring (how many chairs at each table, the plant's height in linking cubes) and descriptions by watching and asking (the color of a leaf, how a crayon feels, which of two class games to play), each recorded on a picture graph or a picture record card. Session 2 (make): groups answer one class question with the mix (is our plant ready to move to a bigger pot: its height in cubes, how its leaves look, how many leaves), choosing a way to collect each piece, and say what each kind told them. Session 3: question cards sorted into questions we can answer by counting or looking (how many windows does our room have) and questions of feeling, guess or imagination (which window has the best view), with the reason said to a partner. Session 4: questions people in the school or town ask to meet a need (when does the bus come, which part of the school garden needs water, how many books does the library need for each class), who each question helps, and how one changed word changes what it asks and who it fits (which game do we like best, which game can all of us play). Out: no question analyzed past counting or looking, no number work past grade 1 math, no computational tool, no complex chart (the picture graph only), no data about a named child, no child answers the community questions (session 4 examines them). End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 27) stands for it. Tools: none (picture graph, record cards, cubes). Curio Class writes: the record cards, the question card sets, the community need pictures, the wording-pair cards, a new scene and question set for the check | After unit 2's attribute sorting (a description is an attribute) and after math grade 1's data unit (see Cross-subject), so the counts and the three-group picture graph are known. The two rows sit in one unit because the collection of session 2 gives children the experience the question sort of session 3 needs. Base: archived Year 2 "Pictograms" lessons 1, 3, 4 and 6 | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "what is an attribute?" step inside session 1's description data, zero added days | Class data is about things, never people: no food, pet, home or body questions (some children have no pet, some meals are short, bodies differ); the teacher picks prompts from neutral lists, and the base's lesson on comparing people is left out for this reason. Town needs are shown in pictures of public places, not of any child's home. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 5 | Computers in our town, then and now. *Where do people use computers, and how did one everyday job change when they arrived?* Anchor: a picture walk down a town street (a library, a clinic, a bus stop, a store, a home doorstep), each place with a device to spot | 4 | g1-computing-around; g1-then-and-now | E1-SOC-CE-16; E1-SOC-HI-14 | Concept area: computing and society. Practices: ESR1 (what each use gives people); IC3 (describe a use, and say what is different now) | Session 1: a picture tour of home, school and town: who uses a computer or a digital device there and for what (a librarian checking out a book, a teacher reading a story on a tablet, a bus stop screen, a store checkout, a doorbell camera, a grown-up calling family on a phone); each child draws one place and one use and says it to a partner. Session 2: one everyday activity at a time (sending a message to someone far away: a letter, then a text message; finding out a fact: a library book, then a search on a device; finding the way: a paper map, then a map on a phone) laid on the two-card timeline, before on the left and after on the right; children say what got easier, faster or different, and one thing that may be harder. Out: no dates, no inventors, no how the device works inside, no named products or companies, no society-wide or money effects, no claim that newer is always better; grade 2 adds people in many places and times who made tools to solve problems (g2-people-solve). End-of-unit (day 31): point to the devices in a new place picture and say who uses one and for what (E1-SOC-CE-16); lay a new activity's before and after cards in order and say one difference (E1-SOC-HI-14); respond day 32 | Last because it gathers the year: the parts of unit 1 and the systems of unit 2 seen in the wider world. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 2, so it can follow the year-end check, which the core check weeks 29 to 32 hold at week 27. Base: archived Year 2 "IT around us" lessons 2, 3 and 4 for session 1; session 2 is our own | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a school worker who uses a device; pictures carry both rows and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | No child is asked about a family member's job, the devices at home or anyone they write to; "someone far away" is shown as a friend who moved, and a child's own offer is welcome. Pictures show many kinds of places and homes, rural and town. Older ways (letters, paper maps, library books) are shown with respect, not as worse; many families use them. Agent reads: sensitivity. |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Course-level check, mid-year (week 13; it is unit 2's end-of-unit assessment) | 2 | 1 | 1 | Day 13 scores unit 2 in full with unseen items and samples unit 1. Unit 2: on paper, order the step-cards of each part of a new pictured job (E1-ALG-PS-01); circle which pictured machine chose by finding a pattern in examples, and draw a new card into the group a pattern-finder would guess (E1-ALG-ML-02); sort new activity cards into people do well, computers do well and both (E1-SOC-HU-15). Spoken, in five groups of six: from two new orders of the same steps, say why the results differ (E1-ALG-IM-03), and give the reason for one activity card's place (E1-SOC-HU-15), recorded as two separate look-fors. Unit 1 sampled: a new share-or-private card with what to do if asked (E1-SYS-SE-12) and a new rule scene judged with its consequence (E1-SYS-IM-13); unit 1's end-of-unit record for E1-SYS-HW-11 is carried unscored. Whole-class paper task of about 15 minutes. Day 14, the respond day for unit 2 and the check, reteaches by error letter and gathers fresh evidence on the missed items |
| Course-level check, year-end (week 27; it is unit 4's end-of-unit assessment) | 4 | 1 | 1 | Day 27 scores unit 4 in full with unseen items and samples units 1 to 3. Unit 4: in the spoken groups, each child collects one count (counting or measuring a new object in cubes) and one description (looking at it) and records both on a record card (E1-DAT-DC-08); on paper, sort new question cards, read aloud, into "count or look to find out" and "a feeling, guess or idea" (E1-DAT-DI-09); spoken, from a new picture of a school need, say who the question helps, and from a new wording pair, say what the changed word changes (E1-DAT-IM-10). Units 1 to 3 sampled with three short new items: what a new event program does when tapped (E1-PRO-RD-06), the first wrong block in a new program, circled and fixed by drawing the moved block (E1-PRO-TR-07), and a new pretend login card (E1-SYS-SE-12); unit 3's end-of-unit record, including the program each child laid out (E1-PRO-PD-04) and the changing-value sort (E1-PRO-VD-05), is carried unscored. Unit 5 follows in weeks 29 to 32 and is checked by its own end-of-unit assessment. Day 28, the respond day for unit 4 and the check, reteaches and records each child's support and gains for grade 2 |

## Cross-subject

Both leans are to math grade 1 in the same year. Math grade 1 meets every school day, so its course days are its
school days (data/plans/calendar.csv); this course's day n falls in week n.

### Mathematics, grade 1

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest math unit that teaches it | Bridge if math teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g1-two-kinds-data | m1-data-three-categories (1.MD.C.4: organize, represent and ask questions of data in three categories) | Unit 4, sessions 1 and 2 (weeks 23 and 24) | math grade 1 unit 1 (days 1 to 16, weeks 1 to 4) | Met before this unit. If a school's math runs late, the class counts each group aloud with the teacher, who writes the numbers, and the picture graph keeps to three groups or fewer |
| g1-data-questions | m1-data-three-categories (1.MD.C.4) | Unit 4, sessions 3 and 4 (weeks 25 and 26) | math grade 1 unit 1 (days 1 to 16), finished by week 4 | Met before this unit. The question sort needs no count; any "how many" answer is counted together with the teacher |

Measuring in cubes (unit 4 sessions 1 and 2, weeks 23 and 24) uses no lean row, but math grade 1 teaches length
units in its unit 6 (days 111 to 128, weeks 23 to 26), the same weeks. If math is later, the unit measures by laying
linking cubes end to end along the plant and counting them together with the teacher, with no gaps or overlaps shown
on the record card, which needs only the counting of math unit 1; comparing taller and shorter directly is
kindergarten math. No row leans on English language arts, science or social studies. Speaking and listening
(explaining a program, following a partner's cards, saying why a question fits) are practiced here, not owned; the
weather and plant examples of unit 3 session 1 and unit 4 are everyday ones and lean on no science row.

## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks (calendar pass).** This course's checks sit in weeks 13 and 27, off the grade 1 core check
   weeks 14 to 16, 19 and 29 to 32. Week 27 is shared with health grades K to 2 (grade 1 year-end); week 13 shares
   with no after-core course as frames.csv stands. It stays for the lead's one calendar pass; the pilot's For the lead
   1 pick applies. Week 26 (arts, SEL) and week 28 (ELD, physical education) are taken too, so the only free week
   before the core year-end weeks is week 25 or earlier, which would need unit 4 to end before unit 3's load allows.
2. **Row changes** (listed in the writer's report with old and new text): g1-data-questions notes (neutral examples in
   place of the pet questions, matching the kindergarten frame's values line), g1-keep-safe notes (signing out and a
   given pretend password, from the E1-SYS-SE-12 boundary; one UK spelling to US), g1-responsible-use notes (the
   consequences for self and class, from the E1-SYS-IM-13 boundary), g1-patterns-ai notes (systems made by people,
   and a suggestion example, from the E1-ALG-ML-02 boundary), g1-two-kinds-data notes (one UK spelling to US).
3. **The picture-block tool's event block.** The kindergarten frame's For the lead 5 asks for a kindergarten to grade 2
   picture-block mode. Grade 1 needs one more block in it: a "when tapped, start" event block, with nothing running
   until the tap, and a step-through run that shows the tap first. Pick: add it to that same build-list line. The
   block-card paper path meets every grade 1 id in full, so the course does not wait on it.
4. **Base units offered by the kindergarten frame.** Kindergarten offered its archived Year 1 "Programming animations"
   unit and "Technology around us" lessons 3 to 5 (mouse and keyboard skills) to this frame. Decided here: neither is
   used. Year 2 "Programming quizzes" carries the event program at the grade 1 age, and mouse and keyboard handling
   has no E1 id (E1-SYS-HW-11 asks for names and purposes, which unit 1 teaches); both stay left out.
5. **Partial base edition and store folder.** As the kindergarten frame's For the lead 4: the Year 2 units mapped here
   exist in the archived edition; the store folder, each file's license line and the per-unit third-party check wait
   on it.

## Source map

The partial base for grade 1 is the archived edition of England's Teach Computing Curriculum, Year 2 (six units of six
lessons; raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived, key stage 1, resources dated 2023; the key stage 1 page lists 12 units
and 72 lessons for Years 1 and 2). Placed from the public unit pages (lesson titles), opened 2026-11-25. The
publisher teaches each unit's lessons in number order; where this frame splits or reorders a unit, the row says why.
Each adapted lesson keeps its idea, is rewritten in our words for an American grade 1, replaces named apps and floor
robots with block cards, a person or toy on the floor grid and our own picture-block tool, and is credited once a
day. Check days are ours, never a kept lesson.

| Base unit (Year 2) | Lessons | Placed in | How it is used | Left out, and why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Computing systems and networks: IT around us | 1 What is IT?; 5 Using IT safely | Unit 1, sessions 1 and 2 | Lesson 5: why rules keep us and our devices safe (session 1); lesson 1: naming the technology and parts around us (session 2), moved after lesson 5 so the rules come first | Lesson 6 (Using IT in different ways): its idea, choosing a technology for a task, has no E1 id beyond what units 1 and 5 teach; to be confirmed against the lesson file when the store folder is made |
| Computing systems and networks: IT around us | 2 IT in school; 3 IT in the world; 4 The benefits of IT | Unit 5, session 1 | Devices at school and in the town, and what each use gives people; moved to the year's end because the society unit closes the year | None |
| Programming A: Robot algorithms | 1 Giving instructions; 6 Break it down; 2 Same but different; 3 Making predictions; 5 Algorithm design | Unit 2, sessions 1 to 4 | Lessons 1 and 6: clear instructions and a job broken into parts (session 1), moved first because the split task is the unit's base; lessons 2 and 3: two algorithms for one goal, predicted and compared (sessions 2 and 3); lesson 5: planning an algorithm for a goal (session 4) | None |
| Programming A: Robot algorithms | 4 Mats and routes | Unit 3, session 4 | Routes on a floor mat as the floor-grid debugging task with block cards | None; the floor robot product is replaced by a person or toy and our picture-block tool |
| Programming B: Programming quizzes | 1 Recap of the block app; 2 Outcomes; 3 Using a design; 4 Changing a design; 6 Evaluating | Unit 3, sessions 2, 3, 5 and 6 | Lesson 1: the block program retrieved (session 2); lesson 2: what a program with a start event does (session 3); lesson 4: changing a working program (session 5); lessons 3 and 6: building from a given design and checking it (session 6) | Lesson 5 (Designing and creating a program): designing one's own algorithm and program is past E1-PRO-PD-04, which asks children to code from a given algorithm; the design part is grade 2 and later |
| Data and information: Pictograms | 1 Counting and comparing; 4 What is an attribute?; 3 Creating pictograms; 6 Presenting information | Unit 4, sessions 1 and 2 | Lessons 1 and 4: counts and descriptions (session 1); lessons 3 and 6: the paper picture graph and answering a question with it (session 2) | Lesson 2 (Enter the data): data entered into an app, and E1-DAT-DC-08 asks for no computational tool; lesson 5 (Comparing people): data about classmates, which the privacy rule keeps out (STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety") |
| Creating media: Digital photography | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: no CSTA id, depends on a camera app and on photographing people and places; outside bar C1's 10 percent if kept |
| Creating media: Making music | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: no CSTA id, depends on a music app |

Units 2 (sessions 5 and 6), 3 (session 1), 4 (sessions 3 and 4) and 5 (session 2) are our own: the partial base has no
lesson on pattern-finding systems, on what people and computers each do well, on values that change, on sorting data
questions or examining community data questions, or on an activity before and after a technology.
