   1: # Course frame: Computer Science, Grade 1
   2: 
   3: Course `computer-science-grade-1` (catalogue id `cs-k12`), grade 1, one year. Draft frame, 2026-11-25, written to the kindergarten pilot's pattern; the scope and sequence sets the days later.
   4: 
   5: ## The course in brief
   6: 
   7: - **Units.** 5, one for each concept area at its grade 1 depth, in the order the ideas build: using our tools well
   8:   (systems and security, so the device rules and the parts' names exist before the first device day of the year),
   9:   plans in parts (algorithms, with the first look at systems that find patterns and at what people and computers each
  10:   do well), code that waits for a tap (programming, built from unit 2's step-card plans), counting, looking and asking
  11:   (data), and computers in our town, then and now (computing and society, the year's closer).
  12: - **Days.** 32 course days: 28 in units, 2 for course-level checks (1 after unit 2, 1 after unit 4) and 2 respond
  13:   days (one after each check). One lesson a week of about 30 minutes, 32 weeks: the computer science default for
  14:   kindergarten to grade 2 (docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md section 2, Cory, Desk q-fix-cs-1), a specials
  15:   rotation, so course day n falls in week n. The 30 minutes hold set-up, clean-up and collecting work: a session opens
  16:   with a three-minute retrieval task from the second session on (APPROACH.md, Pacing), and floor-grid and device
  17:   sessions keep four minutes at the end to put cards, tape and devices away, so a session teaches for about 23
  18:   minutes. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment and end with a respond day (APPROACH.md, Pacing); a
  19:   course-level check that directly follows a unit is that unit's end-of-unit assessment (frame-write.md, After-core
  20:   courses), so in units 2 and 4 the check scores the unit in full and the check's respond day is the unit's respond
  21:   day. Units 1 to 5 teach on 4, 6, 6, 4 and 2 sessions, 22 of the 32. Days follow the load of the rows: unit 2 owns
  22:   four unplugged rows, three of them new ideas at this grade (splitting a task into parts, systems that find patterns,
  23:   people and computers compared), and unit 3 owns four programming rows with device versions, but three of them carry
  24:   kindergarten constructs forward one step (an event, the fix itself), so both get six teaching sessions; unit 5's two
  25:   society rows are single unplugged picture sessions. 32 sessions for 15 rows is the kindergarten count (32 for 14),
  26:   22 of them teaching. Each course-level check takes one check session and one respond session, since a 30-minute
  27:   session holds a whole-class paper task and the spoken answers of five groups. No unit runs over 25 days, so there
  28:   are no mid-unit assessments.
  29: - **Check weeks.** The grade 1 core checks take weeks 14, 15, 16 and 19 (mid-year) and 29 to 32 (year-end)
  30:   (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, target weeks: social studies 14 and 31, science 15 and 29, ELA 16 and 32, math 19 and
  31:   30). This course's checks sit in week 13 (mid-year, after unit 2) and week 27 (year-end, after unit 4), with their
  32:   respond days in weeks 14 and 28 (respond days are not check days). Unit 3 runs in weeks 15 to 22, across the core
  33:   check weeks 15, 16 and 19, because it holds no course-level check; its end-of-unit assessment falls in week 21.
  34:   Unit 5 runs in weeks 29 to 32 after the year-end check, as the kindergarten pilot's unit 5 does; its own
  35:   end-of-unit assessment (week 31) checks its two rows. Overlaps with other after-core subjects' check weeks are the
  36:   lead's calendar pass (For the lead 1).
  37: - **Standard.** The 16 E1 ids of the 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards, all taught here (STANDARD.md section
  38:   2): E1-ALG-PS-01, E1-ALG-ML-02, E1-ALG-IM-03, E1-PRO-PD-04, E1-PRO-VD-05, E1-PRO-RD-06, E1-PRO-TR-07,
  39:   E1-DAT-DC-08, E1-DAT-DI-09, E1-DAT-IM-10, E1-SYS-HW-11, E1-SYS-SE-12, E1-SYS-IM-13, E1-SOC-HI-14, E1-SOC-HU-15,
  40:   E1-SOC-CE-16. Each is taught at the depth its owner row's notes set, in our own words, and each owned id's boundary
  41:   statement was read in the source store's standards PDF (S:\CurioClass-sources\computer-science\csta-2026-11-21,
  42:   read 2026-11-25, never copied or quoted; STANDARD.md "Depth"); "What students learn" says what is out. Plans cite
  43:   ids and state each action in a short phrase of our own (STANDARD.md "Quoting"); the credit line "Standards: 2026
  44:   CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)" goes on any day that leans on a statement. Check set: each
  45:   unit plan checks itself against the computer science bar (docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md part 5, items 1 to 9) and
  46:   the lens (docs/agents/lenses/computer-science.md), and reads the partial base unit it adapts for order; the "Check
  47:   set adds" column names what the writer expects that to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds. No
  48:   Specialty id, and no X + CS id, is claimed.
  49: - **Practices and concept areas.** The Units table's seventh column names the concept area each unit teaches and the
  50:   CSTA practices it teaches inside the content, by code and our label (STANDARD.md section 3), as bar items 1 and 2
  51:   ask; the pairs follow the practices the standards PDF pairs with each owned id. Each unit plan names the practice's
  52:   day and task. Across the year: CT6 define the problem (units 2, 3, 4), CT7 find patterns (units 2, 3, 4), CT8 build
  53:   and plan (units 2, 3), CT9 test, debug and refine (unit 3), IC3 explain and give feedback (every unit), IC5 be a
  54:   reliable teammate (units 1, 3, pair roles), HCD10 learn from and include different users (units 1, 2, 4), ESR1
  55:   weigh who gains (units 1, 2, 4, 5), ESR2 respect people and protect information (unit 1). Dispositions are named in
  56:   the unit plans, not here.
  57: - **Base text.** Partial: England's open computing curriculum, the Teach Computing Curriculum, archived edition
  58:   (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived, key stage 1, resources dated 2023), which its publisher no longer maintains,
  59:   mapped as a fixed text as the kindergarten pilot does. Year 2 is the grade 1 age
  60:   (docs/reviews/computer-science-open-material-2026-11-16.md). License: the scout read the Open Government Licence
  61:   v3.0 "unless expressly stated otherwise" on the publisher's terms page; the archive pages carry no license line of
  62:   their own, so each file's license line is read when the store folder is made. Adapted unit by unit and rephrased for
  63:   an American audience (STANDARD.md section 2, Cory 2026-11-16): US spelling, school years named as US grades, no
  64:   money in pounds, no product or company name, our own browser tool or paper in place of named apps and floor robots.
  65:   Four of its six Year 2 units feed units 1 to 5; its two creating-media units are left out (see Source map). The
  66:   source's name appears only in a day's credit line, once a day.
  67:   Pending(docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md#teach-computing-curriculum): the source store holds no copy of this base, so the
  68:   Source map below rests on the public unit pages (lesson titles, opened 2026-11-25), and each adapted lesson's check
  69:   for third-party images, text and tool screenshots waits on the store folder (kindergarten frame, For the lead 4).
  70: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md (row "teaching day") defines them, in computer science's words (APPROACH.md):
  71:   Understanding is an unplugged or trace session, the first teaching of a construct or idea (a task cut into parts on
  72:   cards, a picture sort, a block program predicted before it runs, a worked program traced). Fluency is a modify
  73:   session: change one step of a working plan or one block of a working program and check it; grade 1 has a little of
  74:   it (unit 2 session 3, unit 3 session 5, and the retrieval openings). Application is a make session: split a new
  75:   task into parts and step-cards a partner carries out (unit 2 session 4), turn a given plan into a block program,
  76:   run it, find and fix what goes wrong (unit 3 session 6), or collect counts and descriptions in more than one way to
  77:   answer a class question (unit 4 session 2). Check is the end-of-unit assessment or a course-level check; Respond
  78:   acts on it by error letter. Inside a unit: understanding before modify before make, and no make day before a trace
  79:   or modify day on the same construct.
  80: - **Evidence.** Grade 1 answers by pointing, sorting, ordering, drawing or saying aloud to a partner; the teacher notes
  81:   by exception on a class checklist (APPROACH.md, "The form of daily evidence"). Every end-of-unit assessment is a
  82:   sitting with unseen items, never the unit's own anchor: a whole-class paper task of about 15 minutes (order a new
  83:   task's step-cards, sort new picture cards, circle the first wrong block and draw the fix, record a count and a
  84:   description) collected from every child, plus one or two spoken answers per child heard in five groups of six
  85:   while the others finish the paper task. Each owned id has its own look-for: a "create" or "debug" id
  86:   (E1-PRO-PD-04, E1-PRO-TR-07) is met by a child laying out or reordering block cards, a "use methods" id
  87:   (E1-DAT-DC-08) by the child collecting, an "explain", "describe" or "examine" id (E1-ALG-IM-03, E1-PRO-RD-06,
  88:   E1-SYS-HW-11, E1-SYS-IM-13, E1-SYS-SE-12, E1-DAT-IM-10, E1-SOC-CE-16) by a spoken answer, and a "recognize",
  89:   "identify", "compare" or "differentiate" id by a sort or a choice on paper with one spoken reason where the id asks
  90:   for one. Units 2 and 4 are assessed in the course-level check that follows them (Days). No score comes from a
  91:   session that first teaches a row. The respond day reteaches by error letter and repeats the missed item in a new
  92:   form.
  93: - **Devices.** One device per pair is the default on a device day, never needed per child. Device days: unit 1
  94:   session 3 (the parts of a real device and their jobs, and signing out), unit 3 sessions 2, 3, 5 and 6 (the
  95:   picture-block tool, with an event block). That leaves about four fifths of the year's time away from a device,
  96:   more than APPROACH.md's "about two thirds" (ours, untested): the E1 rows are written unplugged first, and the
  97:   device days carry the parts that gain from a screen; grade 2 may raise the share as the repeat block arrives.
  98:   Every device day is written four ways (STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms"): one device per pair, a few devices at
  99:   stations with the other pairs on the paper version of the same idea, one screen with a child at the device for each
 100:   step, or no device. No E1 id needs a device to be met in full: the boundary statements ask for no computational
 101:   tool, block cards with a start card on the floor grid meet E1-PRO-PD-04, E1-PRO-RD-06 and E1-PRO-TR-07 in full, and
 102:   the picture cards of device parts meet E1-SYS-HW-11, so the course record never marks a grade 1 id part met for lack
 103:   of a device.
 104: - **Paper path.** Every session runs on paper, cards, tape and objects; the picture-block tool of unit 3 has the
 105:   block-card program beside it (picture block cards with a "when tapped" start card, laid on the same floor grid and
 106:   "run" by a partner when the start card is tapped). No tool asks for a student account, stores a child's name or
 107:   sends anything off the device (STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety"); unit 1's login is a paper pretend login with a
 108:   given pretend password.
 109: - **Reading level and vocabulary.** Grade 1 children are early readers. No code or word is read alone: blocks and
 110:   cards are icons with a short label (start, when tapped), every word is said aloud and shown with a picture or an
 111:   object, and the teacher writes. Each unit names five to eight words taught in depth from its rows' word lists, with
 112:   the everyday words that have a computing meaning taught as two meanings (bug, run, mouse, pattern). Sentence frames:
 113:   "First it ___, then it ___", "I think it will ___ because ___" and "When I tap, it ___". A child may answer with
 114:   arrows, pointing, a drawing or the home language.
 115: - **Swap or leave out.** STANDARD.md section 3 lets a school swap or leave out a named unit for its community's
 116:   reasons. Unit 5 may be left out (homes, places and family life are its ground): E1-SOC-CE-16 and E1-SOC-HI-14 are
 117:   then not claimed that year, and the course still teaches computing and society through E1-SOC-HU-15 in unit 2.
 118:   Unit 4 may be swapped for a different set of neutral class questions and things to measure, not left out, so the
 119:   course always claims E1-DAT-DC-08, E1-DAT-DI-09 and E1-DAT-IM-10 (the bar part 5 fails a course with nothing on
 120:   data). Units 1 to 3 carry the device rules and the models the rest of the year uses and are not swapped or left out.
 121: - **Safety.** No lab or field work, so no laboratory or field safety guide applies. Floor-grid sessions run in a
 122:   cleared space with tape that lies flat, children walk the grid (no running, no blindfolds), and device cords stay
 123:   behind the table; devices are carried with two hands, food and drink stay away from them, and headphones, where a
 124:   school uses them, are set to a low volume by the teacher first. The scope and sequence copies these lines into each
 125:   floor-grid and device day.
 126: - **Costs.** Provisional, for a class of 30, beyond the school's own devices: unit 1 $3 (printing part-and-job cards,
 127:   rule story cards and the paper pretend logins), unit 2 $4 (task part cards, step-card sets, attribute-card sets and
 128:   the people-or-computer activity cards), unit 3 $8 (two rolls of masking tape for a floor grid, printing the block
 129:   cards with start cards and the value picture cards), unit 4 $5 (sticky dots and chart paper for the picture graph,
 130:   record cards, question cards; the linking cubes and objects to measure are classroom items), unit 5 $2 (printing
 131:   the place pictures and the before-and-after activity strips), about $22 in all.
 132: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
 133:   no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Unit 1 has online-safety content (passwords, someone asking for
 134:   one) and disability content (adapted parts shown beside the usual ones: a large-button mouse, a switch, headphones,
 135:   a screen magnifier). Before it reads, the agent studies the pilot's starting pack: the Autistic Self Advocacy
 136:   Network's "Identity-First Language" page (autisticadvocacy.org/about-asan/identity-first-language/), one community's
 137:   own account of language and identity, not taken as every community's; the National Center on Disability and
 138:   Journalism's Disability Language Style Guide (ncdj.org/style-guide/, free, English and Spanish); and, as background
 139:   on accessible hardware only, Section508.gov's "Hardware ICT Overview" (section508.gov/test/ict-hardware-overview/).
 140:   The agent widens the pack to the brief's five sources, two of them owned by the communities whose devices the
 141:   finished cards show, and records them. It then checks the keep-safe section carries health's "If a student tells
 142:   you something" steps word for word (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md; APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground). Unit 2's
 143:   people-or-computer sort touches feelings (comfort a friend, notice a mood): the agent checks no card asks a child
 144:   about their own feelings or a classmate's, and no card says a computer feels or thinks. Units 4 and 5 touch homes,
 145:   families and the town (class data, community needs, devices at home, talking to someone far away): the agent checks
 146:   no question asks about a child's home, food, pets, devices or a family member's work or whereabouts. The course's
 147:   checks get the answer-key agent's read. No unit is hands-on in the laboratory sense, so no safety agent read is
 148:   planned beyond the Safety lines above, which the sensitivity read confirms are on each day.
