 218: | 1 | Using our tools well. *What are the parts of our devices for, and what is my job in keeping them, and us, safe?* Anchor: a class device that "won't work" because a classmate left it signed in, with a juice box beside it and its headphones unplugged | 6 | g1-responsible-use; g1-hardware-parts; g1-keep-safe | E1-SYS-IM-13; E1-SYS-HW-11; E1-SYS-SE-12 | Concept area: systems and security. Practices: ESR1 and IC3 (why a rule helps everyone, said to a partner); IC3 (each part named and its job explained); ESR2 and IC5 (keeping a password private, signing out for the next pair); HCD10 (parts that help different users) | Session 1: the kindergarten device rules retrieved after the hook; why each rule exists, the child's own part in keeping it, and what happens to the child and to the class when it is broken (a dropped device no one can use, a drink near a keyboard, using someone else's sign-in, sound played aloud instead of in headphones), acted out with story cards. Session 2: the parts of a device named with their right words and their purposes on picture cards (screen, keyboard, mouse or touchpad, speaker, headphones, camera, microphone), and that a laptop or tablet runs the apps we use for school work; adapted parts that do the same jobs for different people (a large-button mouse, a switch, a magnifier). Session 3 (device): each pair matches part cards to the parts of a real device and says each one's job, then watches the teacher sign out of the class device on one screen. Session 4: a password is a private key to an account, a lock is its picture; children sign in and out of a paper pretend login with a given pretend password, sort what is fine to share (a drawing) and what stays private (a password, a sign-in picture code), and say what to do if someone asks for it (say no, tell a trusted adult). Out: no internal parts (grade 2 adds input and output, and inside parts come later), no input or output sorting, no child makes up a password and no real account (the boundary asks neither), no copyright, misinformation or digital footprint. End-of-unit (day 5): a new part-and-job card match with one part named and its purpose said aloud (E1-SYS-HW-11); a new rule scene, saying why the rule matters and what happens to self and class if it is broken (E1-SYS-IM-13); a new pretend login card, circling what stays secret and the sign-out button, and saying what to do if asked for it (E1-SYS-SE-12); respond day 6. Tools: device stations (any school device, its home screen only, job: matching parts to jobs; the teacher's sign-out shown on one screen). Curio Class writes: the part-and-job picture cards with adapted parts, the rule story cards, the paper pretend login and the share-or-private sort set | First because the rules and the parts' names must exist before the year's first device turn (session 3) and every device day of unit 3; responsible use opens the unit so the device turn happens under rules the class has just explained. Keep-safe sits with the rules as a rule for keeping our information safe, taught on paper. Base: archived Year 2 "IT around us" lessons 1 and 5 | Candidate: kept, a station rotation plan for a room with three devices on session 3, zero added days | Some children use adapted devices or tools: talk is about what a part lets a person do, never about a classmate; use the wording the sensitivity read settles from its pack. Some homes have no device and some have many: no child is asked what is at home. Passwords: no real password, name or address is said or written; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. Devices carried with two hands, cords behind the table, no food or drink near them, headphone volume set by the teacher. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
 219: | 2 | Plans in parts. *How do we turn a big job into steps someone else can follow, and what can people and computers each do best?* Anchor: the class tries to build the block tower in the picture from one long spoken instruction, and it falls | 6 | g1-split-task; g1-change-steps; g1-patterns-ai; g1-people-or-machine | E1-ALG-PS-01; E1-ALG-IM-03; E1-ALG-ML-02; E1-SOC-HU-15 | Concept area: algorithms and design (with computing and society: people and computers compared). Practices: CT6 (cut the job into smaller parts); CT7 (notice the pattern in the examples); CT8 (plan the steps before carrying them out); ESR1 and IC3 (a changed order gives a different result, said to a partner); HCD10 (which jobs need a person) | Session 1: the step-card retrieved; a familiar multistep job (building the tower, tidying the art table, getting ready for recess) cut into its parts with the teacher, then each part into ordered step-cards, a worked example shown and traced first. Session 2: two step-card sets for the same tower, the steps in different orders; children predict and then build both, and say how the change of order changed the result (a tall steady tower, a short wobbly one). Session 3 (modify): a working plan from session 1 has one step swapped, dropped or added; children predict what will come out differently and check by carrying it out. Session 4 (make): each pair splits a new class job into parts and step-cards, and a partner pair carries the cards out and says whether they worked; the class keeps two plans (a path to the reading rug, a tower) for unit 3. Session 5: attribute cards sorted, then a guessing game: a partner who has seen a few sorted examples guesses a new card's group by the pattern; some computer systems, made by people, look for repeating patterns in many examples to make a choice or make something new (a library screen that suggests a next book from the books read before; a drawing tool that makes a new picture of a cat from the patterns in many cat pictures). Session 6: a sort of activity cards into what people do well (comfort a friend, invent a game, solve a new kind of problem), what a computer does well (add a long list fast, repeat a step without tiring or mistakes, keep a lot of information) and both, with one reason said for each. Out: no conditions (if) and no programming language in the algorithm work, no abstract problems, no kinds of machine learning, no claim that a computer thinks or feels, no machine at all on session 5, no repeat (grade 2), no ethics of who should do a job (later grades). End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 13) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the tower and class-job part and step-cards, the two-order tower sets, the attribute-card sets, the suggestion-screen picture, the people-or-computer activity cards | Second because every later algorithm and program row builds on the split task (g2-algorithm-loops), and unit 3's code is translated from the plans made here (g1-code-sequence's notes name g1-split-task). Change-steps follows the split so there is a plan to change, and the make session comes after the modify. Patterns and people-or-computer close the unit together: the guessing game shows what pattern-finding systems do, and the sort places that beside what people do well. After unit 1, so the year's device rules come first, though this unit needs no device. Base: archived Year 2 "Robot algorithms" lessons 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 for sessions 1 to 4 | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "same but different" pairing of two algorithms for one goal on session 2, zero added days | Feelings cards (comfort a friend, notice a mood) are about people in a story, never about a child in the room. Class jobs, not home jobs: home routines differ, and a home example is only a child's own offer. No food handled. The suggestion screen is a drawn, generic screen with no product name. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
 220: | 3 | Code that waits for a tap. *How do we turn our plan into a program that starts when we tap it, and fix it when it goes wrong?* Anchor: the class's rug path from unit 2, built as picture blocks, does nothing until the teacher taps the start block, then the character walks one square too far | 8 | g1-changing-things; g1-code-sequence; g1-explain-events; g1-fix-sequence | E1-PRO-VD-05; E1-PRO-PD-04; E1-PRO-RD-06; E1-PRO-TR-07 | Concept area: programming. Practices: IC3 and CT7 (name what changes and what stays the same); CT7 and CT8 (turn a plan into blocks); IC3 and CT6 (say what each block does and what the tap starts); CT6 and CT9 (find the first wrong block, change one thing, test again); IC5 (driver and navigator roles that switch at the halfway signal) | Session 1: words for information that changes over time in everyday life (the weather, the temperature outside, the time, a plant's height, a game's score, our age each year) and information that stays the same (a name, a birthday), sorted on picture cards; the character's square changes as a program runs. The first idea of a changing value; the word variable is not taught. Session 2 (device): the worked example: the class's rug-path plan from unit 2 turned into picture blocks with one event block ("when tapped, start") on top, traced block by block and run on one screen, then each pair builds the same program from the plan (or the block-card paper path). Session 3 (device): a short given block program that starts on an event; children say what each block does and what the tap starts, before it runs, then run and compare. Session 4: a given program with one block wrong or out of order (the character jumps before it moves); trace it, find the first block that goes wrong, change one thing and test again: predict, run, find, fix; bug taught with both meanings; errors are normal and shared; on the floor grid with block cards, then checked on one screen. Session 5 (modify, device): a working program; the goal square moves; change one block and test again. Session 6 (make, device): each pair turns a given step-card plan (unit 2's tower or a new picture plan) into a block program with a start block, runs it, fixes what goes wrong and says what they tested. Out: no repeat or loop (grade 2), no conditions, no variables in code and no word variable, no designing the algorithm during the code sessions (the plan is given or made in unit 2), no reading beyond icons and short block labels, no text view for children (shown to the teacher only). End-of-unit (day 21): lay out block cards with a start card for a new given plan (E1-PRO-PD-04); say what a new event program does when tapped, block by block (E1-PRO-RD-06); circle the first wrong block in a new program and move it to fix it (E1-PRO-TR-07); sort new picture cards into changes and stays the same and name one changing thing (E1-PRO-VD-05); respond day 22. Tools: the picture-block tool (large picture blocks on a grid, a "when tapped" event block, a step-through run, job: build, run and fix the program the plan describes; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the block cards and start cards for the paper path, the changes-or-stays-the-same picture cards, the worked, buggy and moved-goal programs with their keys | After unit 2 because the code is translated from unit 2's plans (model_in step-card) and the block program needs the step-card first. The changing-value sort opens the unit so the character's moving square is seen as a value that changes before the code runs. Trace and predict come before fix, fix before modify, and modify before make (APPROACH.md order). Placed across weeks 15 to 22, which include the core check weeks 15, 16 and 19, because it holds no course-level check. Base: archived Year 2 "Programming quizzes" lessons 1 to 4 and 6, and "Robot algorithms" lesson 4 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "changing a design" step as session 5's modify task, zero added days | Floor grid in a cleared space, tape flat, children walk (no running, no blindfolds); a child who uses a wheelchair or cannot walk the grid moves the toy or directs a partner, never sits out. Age is a changing value: say "everyone's age changes each year", and no child's age or birthday is asked or shown. Left and right: hand signs and a shape on each block, never color alone. Devices: two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
 221: | 4 | Counting, looking and asking. *How can we find out about our classroom, and which questions can finding out answer?* Anchor: two classmates argue about whether the class plant has grown, and nobody can say for sure | 4 | g1-two-kinds-data; g1-data-questions | E1-DAT-DC-08; E1-DAT-DI-09; E1-DAT-IM-10 | Concept area: data and analysis. Practices: IC3 and CT7 (say what the count and the description show); CT6 and IC3 (sort the questions and say why); ESR1 and HCD10 (who a question helps and who it leaves out) | Session 1: data collected in more than one way: counts by counting and measuring (how many chairs at each table, the plant's height in linking cubes) and descriptions by watching and asking (the color of a leaf, how a crayon feels, which of two class games to play), each recorded on a picture graph or a picture record card. Session 2 (make): groups answer one class question with the mix (is our plant ready to move to a bigger pot: its height in cubes, how its leaves look, how many leaves), choosing a way to collect each piece, and say what each kind told them. Session 3: question cards sorted into questions we can answer by counting or looking (how many windows does our room have) and questions of feeling, guess or imagination (which window has the best view), with the reason said to a partner. Session 4: questions people in the school or town ask to meet a need (when does the bus come, which part of the school garden needs water, how many books does the library need for each class), who each question helps, and how one changed word changes what it asks and who it fits (which game do we like best, which game can all of us play). Out: no question analyzed past counting or looking, no number work past grade 1 math, no computational tool, no complex chart (the picture graph only), no data about a named child, no child answers the community questions (session 4 examines them). End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 27) stands for it. Tools: none (picture graph, record cards, cubes). Curio Class writes: the record cards, the question card sets, the community need pictures, the wording-pair cards, a new scene and question set for the check | After unit 2's attribute sorting (a description is an attribute) and after math grade 1's data unit (see Cross-subject), so the counts and the three-group picture graph are known. The two rows sit in one unit because the collection of session 2 gives children the experience the question sort of session 3 needs. Base: archived Year 2 "Pictograms" lessons 1, 3, 4 and 6 | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "what is an attribute?" step inside session 1's description data, zero added days | Class data is about things, never people: no food, pet, home or body questions (some children have no pet, some meals are short, bodies differ); the teacher picks prompts from neutral lists, and the base's lesson on comparing people is left out for this reason. Town needs are shown in pictures of public places, not of any child's home. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
 222: | 5 | Computers in our town, then and now. *Where do people use computers, and how did one everyday job change when they arrived?* Anchor: a picture walk down a town street (a library, a clinic, a bus stop, a store, a home doorstep), each place with a device to spot | 4 | g1-computing-around; g1-then-and-now | E1-SOC-CE-16; E1-SOC-HI-14 | Concept area: computing and society. Practices: ESR1 (what each use gives people); IC3 (describe a use, and say what is different now) | Session 1: a picture tour of home, school and town: who uses a computer or a digital device there and for what (a librarian checking out a book, a teacher reading a story on a tablet, a bus stop screen, a store checkout, a doorbell camera, a grown-up calling family on a phone); each child draws one place and one use and says it to a partner. Session 2: one everyday activity at a time (sending a message to someone far away: a letter, then a text message; finding out a fact: a library book, then a search on a device; finding the way: a paper map, then a map on a phone) laid on the two-card timeline, before on the left and after on the right; children say what got easier, faster or different, and one thing that may be harder. Out: no dates, no inventors, no how the device works inside, no named products or companies, no society-wide or money effects, no claim that newer is always better; grade 2 adds people in many places and times who made tools to solve problems (g2-people-solve). End-of-unit (day 31): point to the devices in a new place picture and say who uses one and for what (E1-SOC-CE-16); lay a new activity's before and after cards in order and say one difference (E1-SOC-HI-14); respond day 32 | Last because it gathers the year: the parts of unit 1 and the systems of unit 2 seen in the wider world. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 2, so it can follow the year-end check, which the core check weeks 29 to 32 hold at week 27. Base: archived Year 2 "IT around us" lessons 2, 3 and 4 for session 1; session 2 is our own | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a school worker who uses a device; pictures carry both rows and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | No child is asked about a family member's job, the devices at home or anyone they write to; "someone far away" is shown as a friend who moved, and a child's own offer is welcome. Pictures show many kinds of places and homes, rural and town. Older ways (letters, paper maps, library books) are shown with respect, not as worse; many families use them. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
