 275: ## Source map
 276: 
 277: The partial base for grade 1 is the archived edition of England's Teach Computing Curriculum, Year 2 (six units of six
 278: lessons; raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived, key stage 1, resources dated 2023; the key stage 1 page lists 12 units
 279: and 72 lessons for Years 1 and 2). Placed from the public unit pages (lesson titles), opened 2026-11-25. The
 280: publisher teaches each unit's lessons in number order; where this frame splits or reorders a unit, the row says why.
 281: Each adapted lesson keeps its idea, is rewritten in our words for an American grade 1, replaces named apps and floor
 282: robots with block cards, a person or toy on the floor grid and our own picture-block tool, and is credited once a
 283: day. Check days are ours, never a kept lesson.
 284: 
 285: | Base unit (Year 2) | Lessons | Placed in | How it is used | Left out, and why |
 286: |---|---|---|---|---|
 287: | Computing systems and networks: IT around us | 1 What is IT?; 5 Using IT safely | Unit 1, sessions 1 and 2 | Lesson 5: why rules keep us and our devices safe (session 1); lesson 1: naming the technology and parts around us (session 2), moved after lesson 5 so the rules come first | Lesson 6 (Using IT in different ways): its idea, choosing a technology for a task, has no E1 id beyond what units 1 and 5 teach; to be confirmed against the lesson file when the store folder is made |
 288: | Computing systems and networks: IT around us | 2 IT in school; 3 IT in the world; 4 The benefits of IT | Unit 5, session 1 | Devices at school and in the town, and what each use gives people; moved to the year's end because the society unit closes the year | None |
 289: | Programming A: Robot algorithms | 1 Giving instructions; 6 Break it down; 2 Same but different; 3 Making predictions; 5 Algorithm design | Unit 2, sessions 1 to 4 | Lessons 1 and 6: clear instructions and a job broken into parts (session 1), moved first because the split task is the unit's base; lessons 2 and 3: two algorithms for one goal, predicted and compared (sessions 2 and 3); lesson 5: planning an algorithm for a goal (session 4) | None |
 290: | Programming A: Robot algorithms | 4 Mats and routes | Unit 3, session 4 | Routes on a floor mat as the floor-grid debugging task with block cards | None; the floor robot product is replaced by a person or toy and our picture-block tool |
 291: | Programming B: Programming quizzes | 1 Recap of the block app; 2 Outcomes; 3 Using a design; 4 Changing a design; 6 Evaluating | Unit 3, sessions 2, 3, 5 and 6 | Lesson 1: the block program retrieved (session 2); lesson 2: what a program with a start event does (session 3); lesson 4: changing a working program (session 5); lessons 3 and 6: building from a given design and checking it (session 6) | Lesson 5 (Designing and creating a program): designing one's own algorithm and program is past E1-PRO-PD-04, which asks children to code from a given algorithm; the design part is grade 2 and later |
 292: | Data and information: Pictograms | 1 Counting and comparing; 4 What is an attribute?; 3 Creating pictograms; 6 Presenting information | Unit 4, sessions 1 and 2 | Lessons 1 and 4: counts and descriptions (session 1); lessons 3 and 6: the paper picture graph and answering a question with it (session 2) | Lesson 2 (Enter the data): data entered into an app, and E1-DAT-DC-08 asks for no computational tool; lesson 5 (Comparing people): data about classmates, which the privacy rule keeps out (STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety") |
 293: | Creating media: Digital photography | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: no CSTA id, depends on a camera app and on photographing people and places; outside bar C1's 10 percent if kept |
 294: | Creating media: Making music | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: no CSTA id, depends on a music app |
