tures). Session 6: a sort of activity cards into what people do well (comfort a friend, invent a game, solve a new kind of problem), what a computer does well (add a long list fast, repeat a step without tiring or mistakes, keep a lot of information) and both, with one reason said for each. Out: no conditions (if) and no programming language in the algorithm work, no abstract problems, no kinds of machine learning, no claim that a computer thinks or feels, no machine at all on session 5, no repeat (grade 2), no ethics of who should do a job (later grades). End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 13) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the tower and class-job part and step-cards, the two-order tower sets, the attribute-card sets, the suggestion-screen picture, the people-or-computer activity cards | Second because every later algorithm and program row builds on the split task (g2-algorithm-loops), and unit 3's code is translated from the plans made here (g1-code-sequence's notes name g1-split-task). Change-steps follows the split so there is a plan to change, and the make session comes after the modify. Patterns and people-or-computer close the unit together: the guessing game shows what pattern-finding systems do, and the sort places that beside what people do well. After unit 1, so the year's device rules come first, though this unit needs no device. Base: archived Year 2 "Robot algorithms" lessons 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 for sessions 1 to 4 | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "same but different" pairing of two algorithms for one goal on session 2, zero added days | Feelings cards (comfort a friend, notice a mood) are about people in a story, never about a child in the room. Class jobs, not home jobs: home routines differ, and a home example is only a child's own offer. No food handled. The suggestion screen is a drawn, generic screen with no product name. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
