uare moves; change one block and test again. Session 6 (make, device): each pair turns a given step-card plan (unit 2's tower or a new picture plan) into a block program with a start block, runs it, fixes what goes wrong and says what they tested. Out: no repeat or loop (grade 2), no conditions, no variables in code and no word variable, no designing the algorithm during the code sessions (the plan is given or made in unit 2), no reading beyond icons and short block labels, no text view for children (shown to the teacher only). End-of-unit (day 21): lay out block cards with a start card for a new given plan (E1-PRO-PD-04); say what a new event program does when tapped, block by block (E1-PRO-RD-06); circle the first wrong block in a new program and move it to fix it (E1-PRO-TR-07); sort new picture cards into changes and stays the same and name one changing thing (E1-PRO-VD-05); respond day 22. Tools: the picture-block tool (large picture blocks on a grid, a "when tapped" event block, a step-through run, job: build, run and fix the program the plan describes; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the block cards and start cards for the paper path, the changes-or-stays-the-same picture cards, the worked, buggy and moved-goal programs with their keys | After unit 2 because the code is translated from unit 2's plans (model_in step-card) and the block program needs the step-card first. The changing-value sort opens the unit so the character's moving square is seen as a value that changes before the code runs. Trace and predict come before fix, fix before modify, and modify before make (APPROACH.md order). Placed across weeks 15 to 22, which include the core check weeks 15, 16 and 19, because it holds no course-level check. Base: archived Year 2 "Programming quizzes" lessons 1 to 4 and 6, and "Robot algorithms" lesson 4 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "changing a design" step as session 5's modify task, zero added days | Floor grid in a cleared space, tape flat, children walk (no running, no blindfolds); a child who uses a wheelchair or cannot walk the grid moves the toy or directs a partner, never sits out. Age is a changing value: say "everyone's age changes each year", and no child's age or birthday is asked or shown. Left and right: hand signs and a shape on each block, never color alone. Devices: two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 4 | Counting, looking and asking. *How can we find out about our classroom, and which questions can finding out answer?* Anchor: two classmates argue about whether the class plant has grown, and nobody can say for sure | 4 | g1-two-kinds-data; g1-data-questions | E1-DAT-DC-08; E1-DAT-DI-09; E1-DAT-IM-10 | Concept area: data and analysis. Practices: IC3 and CT7 (say what the count and the description show); CT6 and IC3 (sort the questions and say why); ESR1 and HCD10 (who a question helps and who it leaves out) | Session 1: data collected in more than one way: counts by counting and measuring (how many chairs at each table, the plant's height in linking cubes) and descriptions by watching and asking (the color of a leaf, how a crayon feels, which of two class games to play), each recorded on a picture graph or a picture record card. Session 2 (make): groups answer one class question with the mix (is our plant ready to move to a bigger pot: its height in cubes, how its leaves look, how many leaves), choosing a way to collect each piece, and say what each kind told them. Session 3: question cards sorted into questions we can answer by counting or looking (how many windows does our room have) and questions of feeling, guess or imagination (which window has the best view), with the reason said to a partner. Session 4: questions people in the school or town ask to meet a need (when does the bus come, which part of the school garden needs water, how many books does the library need for each class), who each question helps, and how one changed word changes what it asks and who it fits (which game do we like best, which game can all of us play). Out: no question analyzed past counting or looking, no number work past grade 1 math, no computational tool, no complex chart (the picture graph only), no data about a named child, no child answers the community questions (session 4 examines them). End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 27) stands for it. Tools: none (picture graph, record cards, cubes). Curio Class writes: the record cards, the question card sets, the community need pictures, the wording-pair cards, a new scene and question set for the check | After unit 2's attribute sorting (a description is an attribute) and after math grade 1's data unit (see Cross-subject), so the counts and the three-group picture graph are known. The two rows sit in one unit because the collection of session 2 gives children the experience the question sort of session 3 needs. Base: archived Year 2 "Pictograms" lessons 1, 3, 4 and 6 | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "what is an attribute?" step inside session 1's description data, zero added days | Class data is about things, never people: no food, pet, home or body questions (some children have no pet, some meals are short, bodies differ); the teacher picks prompts from neutral lists, and the base's lesson on comparing people is left out for this reason. Town needs are shown in pictures of public places, not of any child's home. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 5 | Computers in our town, then and now. *Where do people use computers, and how did one everyday job change when they arrived?* Anchor: a picture walk down a town street (a library, a clinic, a bus stop, a store, a home doorstep), each place with a device to spot | 4 | g1-computing-around; g1-then-and-now | E1-SOC-CE-16; E1-SOC-HI-14 | Concept area: computing and society. Practices: ESR1 (what each use gives people); IC3 (describe a use, and say what is different now) | Session 1: a picture tour of home, school and town: who uses a computer or a digital device there and for what (a librarian checking out a book, a teacher reading a story on a tablet, a bus stop screen, a store checkout, a doorbell camera, a grown-up calling family on a phone); each child draws one place and one use and says it to a partner. Session 2: one everyday activity at a time (sending a message to someone far away: a letter, then a text message; finding out a fact: a library book, then a search on a device; finding the way: a paper map, then a map on a phone) laid on the two-card timeline, before on the left and after on the right; children say what got easier, faster or different, and one thing that may be harder. Out: no dates, no inventors, no how the device works inside, no named products or companies, no society-wide or money effects, no claim that newer is always better; grade 2 adds people in many places and times who made tools to solve problems (g2-people-solve). End-of-unit (day 31): point to the devices in a new place picture and say who uses one and for what (E1-SOC-CE-16); lay a new activity's before and after cards in order and say one difference (E1-SOC-HI-14); respond day 32 | Last because it gathers the year: the parts of unit 1 and the systems of unit 2 seen in the wider world. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 2, so it can follow the year-end check, which the core check weeks 29 to 32 hold at week 27. Base: archived Year 2 "IT around us" lessons 2, 3 and 4 for session 1; session 2 is our own | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a school worker who uses a device; pictures carry both rows and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | No child is asked about a family member's job, the devices at home or anyone they write to; "someone far away" is shown as a friend who moved, and a child's own offer is welcome. Pictures show many kinds of places and homes, rural and town. Older ways (letters, paper maps, library books) are shown with respect, not as worse; many families use them. Agent reads: sensitivity. |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Course-level check, mid-year (week 13; it is unit 2's end-of-unit assessment) | 2 | 1 | 1 | Day 13 scores unit 2 in full with unseen items and samples unit 1. Unit 2: on paper, order the step-cards of each part of a new pictured job (E1-ALG-PS-01); circle which pictured machine chose by finding a pattern in examples, and draw a new card into the group a pattern-finder would guess (E1-ALG-ML-02); sort new activity cards into people do well, computers do well and both (E1-SOC-HU-15). Spoken, in five groups of six: from two new orders of the same steps, say why the results differ (E1-ALG-IM-03), and give the reason for one activity card's place (E1-SOC-HU-15), recorded as two separate look-fors. Unit 1 sampled: a new share-or-private card with what to do if asked (E1-SYS-SE-12) and a new rule scene judged with its consequence (E1-SYS-IM-13); unit 1's end-of-unit record for E1-SYS-HW-11 is carried unscored. Whole-class paper task of about 15 minutes. Day 14, the respond day for unit 2 and the check, reteaches by error letter and gathers fresh evidence on the missed items |
| Course-level check, year-end (week 27; it is unit 4's end-of-unit assessment) | 4 | 1 | 1 | Day 27 scores unit 4 in full with unseen items and samples units 1 to 3. Unit 4: in the spoken groups, each child collects one count (counting or measuring a new object in cubes) and one description (looking at it) and records both on a record card (E1-DAT-DC-08); on paper, sort new question cards, read aloud, into "count or look to find out" and "a feeling, guess or idea" (E1-DAT-DI-09); spoken, from a new picture of a school need, say who the question helps, and from a new wording pair, say what the changed word changes (E1-DAT-IM-10). Units 1 to 3 sampled with three short new items: what a new event program does when tapped (E1-PRO-RD-06), the first wrong block in a new program, circled and fixed by drawing the moved block (E1-PRO-TR-07), and a new pretend login card (E1-SYS-SE-12); unit 3's end-of-unit record, including the program each child laid out (E1-PRO-PD-04) and the changing-value sort (E1-PRO-VD-05), is carried unscored. Unit 5 follows in weeks 29 to 32 and is checked by its own end-of-unit assessment. Day 28, the respond day for unit 4 and the check, reteaches and records each child's support and gains for grade 2 |

## Cross-subject

Both leans are to math grade 1 in the same year. Math grade 1 meets every school day, so its course days are its
school days (data/plans/calendar.csv); this course's day n falls in week n.

### 5. Computer science

**Rests on.** Ohio's rubric (April 2025): gateway 1 read in full by the writer, all 24 pages by the expert.

1. [S] Coverage spans every concept area, not programming alone: systems, data, algorithms, society.
2. [S] The practices (working together, testing and fixing, abstraction, explaining) are taught inside the content.
3. [I] Programming ideas are ordered, and each band says what the one before taught.
4. [I] Students meet real, current uses and cases of computing.
5. [O] Every unit has unplugged work. Ohio asks about offline versions and gives them no points.
6. [I] Technology is built into the learning, not added on: each tool has a purpose in the lesson, gives feedback,
   and does not distract. This is where Ohio's first gateway puts most of its points.
7. [I] Tools run in a common browser on school devices, with notes for technical staff. [O] Nothing to install.
8. [I] Assessment includes projects and code, with rubrics.
9. [I] Teacher pages assume a teacher not trained in the subject and explain the content at adult level.

**Fails if:** block puzzles with no concepts named; nothing on data or society; a tool that needs an install or an
account; no help for the non-specialist.

**Unsupported.** No national materials rubric. No page may claim coding improves general thinking.

**Standards.** Loaded (data/standards/): the 196 Foundational ids, the 135 Specialty ids and the statements; see docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md. The four high school courses teach the Foundational set and claim a
Specialty standard only where a topic already goes that far (decided 2026-11-11, card q-standards-currency): eight S1 ids, four in grade 11 and four in grade 12 (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1; named in docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md).

**Scope and sequence states:** the concept areas per unit. **Section plan fills:** the unplugged task.
**Confidence:** likely.

strand,grade,skill_id,standard,assumes,hands_on,model_in,model_out,words,cross_subject,notes
algorithms,K,k-follow-steps,EK-ALG-PS-01,,g1-split-task,,step-card,step; first; next; last; order; follow; plan; algorithm,,"Unplugged day. Children carry out everyday routines as ordered steps (washing hands, getting dressed, a snack line) and a made-up one (a robot dance), saying first, next, last. The word algorithm is taught in context: a set of steps in order that we follow is an algorithm. Introduces the step-card: one picture per step, laid left to right. Words are spoken and shown with pictures; nothing is read alone. This is the base row for every algorithm row after it."
algorithms,K,k-people-steps,EK-ALG-IM-03; EK-SOC-HU-15,,g1-change-steps; g1-people-or-machine,step-card,step-card,make; design; invent; people; helper; rule,,"Unplugged day. A person made each routine and each game rule, so a person can change it: the class changes one step of a working step-card set and checks what happens. With the teacher, children describe in simple words how a person makes an algorithm: choose a task, notice its smaller parts, put the steps in order to reach the goal (breaking a task into parts on one's own is grade 1's g1-split-task). Then children make a step-card set for a game they know, and a classmate follows it and says whether it worked. Tells the class that people design the tools around them (a spoon, a pencil, a game, a computer) and the steps they run: a person thought of a game's rules, drew its characters and programmed it. Part of this row is met by talk and drawing; no device needed."
programming,K,k-sequence-commands,EK-PRO-PD-04,,g1-code-sequence,step-card,step-card; block-program,command; program; forward; turn; left; right; blocks,math:mk-shapes-positions,"Unplugged first: a child moves a partner or a toy along a floor grid using arrow cards, then lays the cards in order as a program. Device day: the same short path built from large picture blocks in a block language, one block per step, run and watched. Introduces the block-program. A worked path is shown and traced before any child builds alone. Device versions follow STANDARD.md (one device per pair, stations, one screen, or paper arrow cards)."
programming,K,k-describe-sequence,EK-PRO-RD-06,,g1-explain-events,step-card; block-program,step-card; block-program,tell; describe; does; happens; because,,"Given a short block program or an arrow-card line, children say aloud what it makes the character do, step by step, before it runs, then check by running or by walking it. Teacher notes the answer by exception. Reading is by picture only. Unplugged day with a device check; the paper path needs no screen."
programming,K,k-wrong-step,EK-PRO-TR-07,,g1-fix-sequence,step-card; block-program,step-card; block-program,bug; fix; wrong; broken; try again; test,,"A given program that does not reach the goal (the toy stops short, turns the wrong way). Children point to the step where it first goes wrong and say what to change. Bug is taught with both meanings, an insect and a mistake in a program. Errors are normal and shared. Unplugged with arrow cards, or device day with a one-block fix."
programming,K,k-symbols,EK-PRO-VD-05,,g1-changing-things,,,sign; symbol; gesture; thumbs up; traffic light; means; choose,,"Unplugged day. Everyday signs and gestures carry information that people use to choose: a red light, a thumbs up, a wave, an arrow, a clock hand. Children read pictured signs and gesture cards and say what each tells them to do. This is the first idea of a value that stands for something; no variable is taught. Spoken and pictured words only."
algorithms,K,k-sort-attributes,EK-ALG-ML-02,,g1-patterns-ai,,attribute-cards,group; same; different; color; shape; size; pattern; sort,math:mk-sort-count,"Unplugged day. Children sort real objects and picture cards by one attribute (color, size, shape, wet or dry), find what the group has in common and then guess which group a new card belongs to. This is how machines that sort things first work, taught without the machine: notice what is the same, then decide. Introduces the attribute-cards. No device needed."
data,K,k-ask-and-count,EK-DAT-DI-09; EK-DAT-DC-08,,g1-two-kinds-data; g1-data-questions,,picture-graph,question; count; more; fewer; most; graph; answer,math:mk-sort-count,"Unplugged day. The class asks a question about its own room (which game, which weather, which class-library book) and collects the answers by placing a block or sticker per child on a floor or wall picture graph, then reads it to answer. The teacher poses the first question; children pose the next one from a picture prompt. Introduces the picture-graph. Class data is about things, tallied by hand; no child is named. Used again with every later data row."
data,K,k-data-decide,EK-DAT-IM-10,,g1-data-questions,picture-graph,picture-graph,choose; decide; because; plan; vote,math:mk-sort-count,"Unplugged day. The class uses its own picture graph to make a real choice (which game to play, which book to read) and says what the graph showed. Children name one other everyday decision that people make from counts (what to pack when it rains, what to cook). Said aloud and pointed to; teacher notes by exception."
systems,K,k-tools-jobs,EK-SYS-HW-11,,g1-hardware-parts,,,tool; job; use; computer; tablet; button; screen; touch,,"Unplugged and device day. Children match tools to jobs for different people (a spoon and a fork, a magnifier, a pencil grip, a screen reader, a keyboard) and name the parts of a device they touch or look at. Asks who a tool works for and who it does not, the first human-centered idea. Device version: a few devices at stations, or one screen with a child at the device for each step."
systems,K,k-device-manners,EK-SYS-IM-13,,g1-responsible-use,,,careful; share; turn; ask; kind; safe; rules,,"Unplugged and device day. The class makes simple rules for using shared devices and tools (take turns, ask before using, handle with two hands, stop when told) and acts out a right way and a wrong way. Children say why each rule helps people. Said aloud; no reading."
systems,K,k-public-private,EK-SYS-SE-12,,g1-keep-safe,,,public; private; share; secret; name; address; password,,"Unplugged day. Children sort picture cards into what is fine to tell anyone (a favorite color) and what only family or a trusted adult should know (a home address, a password). No real personal detail is used or asked for. Introduces the idea that private things need protecting, which later rows turn into accounts and passwords."
society,K,k-devices-places,EK-SOC-CE-16,,g1-computing-around,,,home; school; work; use; device; helper; job,,"Unplugged day. Children point out and name devices in pictures of a home, a school and a workplace (a store checkout, a clinic, a farm), and say who uses each and for what. No named products. Builds the sense that computing is part of many jobs."
society,K,k-then-now,EK-SOC-HI-14,,g1-then-and-now,,timeline,before; after; then; now; change; old; new; long ago,,"Unplugged day. Paired pictures of an older and a newer version of a computing device used at school or at home (a corded phone with push buttons and a flat touch-screen phone, a deep box-shaped screen and a flat screen, a large desk computer and a tablet) are put in order on a two-card strip, older on the left. Children say what looks different (smaller, flatter, no cord, a touch screen). Introduces the timeline as a short strip of then and now. No dates, inventors or how the parts work inside; comparing how an everyday activity was done before and after a technology arrived is grade 1's g1-then-and-now. No child is asked about the devices at home; a child's own offer is welcome."
algorithms,1,g1-split-task,E1-ALG-PS-01,k-follow-steps,g2-algorithm-loops,step-card,step-card,break down; part; whole; step; list; task; goal,,"Unplugged day. A bigger task (make a sandwich, tidy the room, build a block tower) is broken into small parts and each part into steps on cards. Children check by having a partner carry the cards out. Builds on the K step-card by adding the idea of parts of a task. The cards become the plan for the next programming row. Device version: not required; the idea is met unplugged."
algorithms,1,g1-change-steps,E1-ALG-IM-03,k-people-steps,g2-algorithm-peers,step-card,step-card,change; different; result; swap; same; instead,,"Unplugged day. A working step-card plan is changed (swap two steps, drop one, add one) and children predict what comes out differently, then check by carrying it out. Explains in a sentence that a change to the steps leads to a different result. Builds on K: a person made the plan, so a person can change it."
algorithms,1,g1-patterns-ai,E1-ALG-ML-02,k-sort-attributes,g2-ai-training-data,attribute-cards,attribute-cards,pattern; example; guess; sort; machine; learn; picture; data,,"Unplugged day. Children sort picture cards, then play a guessing game where a partner who has seen a few examples guesses which group a new card is in by the pattern. Names the idea that some computer systems find patterns in many examples to make a decision or make something new. Said simply, with no claim that a machine thinks. The attribute-cards from K carry on."
data,1,g1-two-kinds-data,E1-DAT-DC-08,k-ask-and-count,g2-data-types,picture-graph,picture-graph,number; count; word; describe; collect; ask; watch; record,math:m1-data-three-categories,"Unplugged day. The class collects both counts (how many chairs, how many sunny days) and descriptions (the color of a leaf, how a crayon feels) using asking, watching and measuring, and records each on a picture graph or a labelled card. Uses more than one way to collect, then answers a question with the mix. Number and word data are named as types in grade 2."
data,1,g1-data-questions,E1-DAT-DI-09; E1-DAT-IM-10,k-ask-and-count; k-data-decide,g2-data-questions,picture-graph,picture-graph,question; data; ask; answer; need; community; family,math:m1-data-three-categories,"Unplugged day. Children sort questions into those that counting can answer (how many pets does the class have) and those that need something else (which pet is best). Then they look at questions people in the school or town might ask to meet a need (when does the bus come, which garden needs water). Data work stays on class things, never on named children. They also see how changing one word changes what a question asks and who it fits (do you have a pet, do you look after a pet)."
programming,1,g1-code-sequence,E1-PRO-PD-04,k-sequence-commands,g2-code-loops,step-card; block-program,step-card; block-program,code; event; start; when; sequence; character,,"Device day, with an unplugged day first. Children turn a step-card plan from g1-split-task into blocks, including one event block (when this is tapped, start), and run it. The worked example is shown and traced before anyone builds alone. Paper stand-in: arrow cards with a start card. A text view of the same program is shown to the teacher only."
programming,1,g1-explain-events,E1-PRO-RD-06,k-describe-sequence,g2-explain-build,block-program,block-program,event; tap; press; what happens; because; order,,"Children read a short given block program that starts on an event and say what it does and why, before running it. Predict, then run, then compare. The K idea that order matters returns with an event: nothing happens until the event. Spoken or pointed answers; no reading beyond icons."
programming,1,g1-fix-sequence,E1-PRO-TR-07,k-wrong-step,g2-debug-loops,block-program; step-card,block-program; step-card,debug; bug; find; fix; test; again; first wrong,,"A given block program with one wrong step or one out of order. Children trace it, find the first step that is wrong, change one thing, and test again. This is the debugging method in four words: predict, run, find, fix. Unplugged with arrow cards on a floor grid, or device day."
programming,1,g1-changing-things,E1-PRO-VD-05,k-symbols,g2-name-and-label,,,change; stay the same; day; weather; height; age; count; time,,"Unplugged day. Children sort pictured things by whether the value changes over time (the weather, a plant's height, the date, their age) or stays the same (a birthday, a name). This is a first look at a changing value without a program. Examples use plants, weather and clocks, not named people."
society,1,g1-computing-around,E1-SOC-CE-16,k-devices-places,g2-interests-careers,,,community; library; shop; clinic; use; digital; school,,"Unplugged day. A picture tour of home, school and town: who uses a computer or a digital device there, and for what (checking out a book, paying, a map, a doorbell). Children draw one place and one use. No named products or companies."
society,1,g1-then-and-now,E1-SOC-HI-14,k-then-now,g2-people-solve,timeline,timeline,before; after; compare; change; activity; invention; long ago,,"Unplugged day. Take one everyday activity (talking to a faraway friend, finding a place, listening to music) and lay out how it was done before and after a technology arrived on the two-card timeline. Children say what got easier, faster or different. Facts about dates are from memory and are checked by the teacher before use."
society,1,g1-people-or-machine,E1-SOC-HU-15,k-people-steps,g2-people-solve,,,good at; people; computer; fast; tired; creative; check; kind,,"Unplugged day. Sort a set of activities into what people do well (comfort a friend, invent a game, notice a mood) and what a computer does well (add a long list fast, repeat the same step without tiring, store a lot). Some activities go in both. No claim that computers feel or think."
systems,1,g1-hardware-parts,E1-SYS-HW-11,k-tools-jobs,g2-io-parts,,,keyboard; mouse; screen; speaker; camera; hardware; part; job,,"Unplugged and device day. Children name the basic parts of a device (screen, keyboard, mouse or touchpad, speaker, camera, microphone) with the right words and say each part's purpose. Match part to job on a pictured device and on the real one. One device per pair, or one on the class screen."
systems,1,g1-responsible-use,E1-SYS-IM-13,k-device-manners,g2-benefits-harms,,,responsible; careful; share; ask; rules; kind; time,,"Unplugged day. Children say what a responsible user does with shared devices and tools: asks first, takes turns, keeps things clean and safe, tells an adult when something seems wrong. Acted out with a short story card, no real accounts. Builds on the K rules."
systems,1,g1-keep-safe,E1-SYS-SE-12,k-public-private,g2-online-consequences,,,password; account; lock; safe; secret; share; adult,,"Unplugged day. Introduces a password as a private key to an account and a lock as a picture. Children practise a pretend login on paper, sort what to share (a drawing) and what to keep private (a password), and say what to do if someone asks for it. No real account is made by any child."
algorithms,2,g2-algorithm-loops,E2-ALG-PS-01,g1-split-task,g3-algorithm-combo,step-card,step-card,repeat; loop; again; times; event; when; algorithm,math:m2-arrays-equal-addends,"Unplugged day. Children write an algorithm that has an ordered sequence, one event (when the bell rings) and a repeat (clap three times) for a dance, a drawing or a game, then a partner follows it. The repeat shortens the step-cards: a card says repeat 3 and holds the steps that repeat. Builds on splitting a task into parts. Device version: not required."
algorithms,2,g2-algorithm-peers,E2-ALG-IM-03,g1-change-steps,g3-algorithm-effects,step-card,step-card,fair; peer; different; people; needs; rule; game,,"Unplugged day. A game's rules are an algorithm. Children play a game as written and notice who it works well for and who it leaves out (the rule that needs a fast runner), then say whether everyone had a fair chance and whether anyone might never get a turn. The same rule can work well for one child and badly for another. Judging fairness is the skill; designing a fairer rule is not asked in grade 2."
algorithms,2,g2-ai-training-data,E2-ALG-ML-02,g1-patterns-ai,g3-ml-new-data,attribute-cards,training-set,train; training; example; data; label; model; guess; wrong,,"Unplugged day. A class sorter learns from labelled example cards: students act as the machine, look at the labelled examples (the training set) and then guess new cards. Children see that a guess is only as good as the examples it learned from, for instance when every example of a bird has wings spread. Introduces the training-set. A device sorting tool may be shown on one screen."
